No. In stateless mode you should create a new `McpServer` and a new `StreamableHTTPServerTransport` for each request. You can share the expensive parts across requests, like DB pools, config and clients.

**Why:** Sharing those instances caused a real security bug, **GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7 / CVE-2026-25536** (CVSS 7.1). It covers two related problems:

1. **One transport reused across requests.** This was most common in stateless mode, where there's no `sessionIdGenerator`. The transport tracks which HTTP stream each JSON-RPC request ID belongs to. Clients number their messages from 0, so two clients sending at the same time can use the same ID. One client's response can then go to the other client.
2. **One `McpServer` reused across several transports.** A server holds only one active transport connection, so responses can go to the wrong client in the same way.

The fix is in **1.26.0**, which is the version to be on. The affected range starts at 1.10.0 according to NVD, but at 1.25.2 according to some other databases. As I understand it, newer versions also throw an error if you reuse a connected server or a stateless transport, instead of quietly sending data to the wrong client. I didn't confirm that in the release notes, so check your version.

**Pattern (v1.x imports):**

```ts
import express from "express";
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StreamableHTTPServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/streamableHttp.js";

// Shared, stateless-safe resources live at module scope
const db = createPool();

function buildServer() {
  const server = new McpServer({ name: "my-server", version: "1.0.0" });
  server.registerTool("lookup", { /* schema */ }, async (args) => {
    /* use db */
  });
  return server;
}

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

app.post("/mcp", async (req, res) => {
  const server = buildServer();
  const transport = new StreamableHTTPServerTransport({ sessionIdGenerator: undefined });
  res.on("close", () => {
    transport.close();
    server.close();
  });
  await server.connect(transport);
  await transport.handleRequest(req, res, req.body);
});
```

Building a server for each request costs little, since registering tools is just storing handlers in a map. If you need to share one server and transport, use stateful mode instead, with one transport per session ID. In v2 the SDK is split into separate packages (server, node, client, core), so the import paths will be different.

Sources:
- [NVD – CVE-2026-25536](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-25536)
- [OSV – GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7)
- [GitLab Advisory – CVE-2026-25536](https://advisories.gitlab.com/pkg/npm/@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/CVE-2026-25536/)
- [Mend – CVE-2026-25536](https://bolt.whitesourcesoftware.com/vulnerability-database/CVE-2026-25536)
- [cvelogic – CVE-2026-25536](https://www.cvelogic.com/cve/CVE-2026-25536)
- [Cloudflare Agents – MCP handler API](https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/model-context-protocol/apis/handler-api/index.md)

